  ($rw4$cmr7h3bb700271yx3ujnp5ov1$rw4$::text, $rw4$nursing$rw4$::text, $rw4$psychosocial$rw4$::text, $rw4$A 54-year-old man with type 2 diabetes is admitted for uncontrolled blood glucose. His home metformin and glipizide are on hold. Current orders: insulin glargine 20 units subcutaneously daily (given last evening), insulin aspart 6 units subcutaneously before meals, and a correction scale to add to the meal dose (151-200 mg/dL add 1 unit; 201-250 mg/dL add 2 units; 251-300 mg/dL add 4 units). At 0730 his point-of-care glucose is 250 mg/dL and his breakfast tray has arrived. He reports fatigue and thirst. He is alert, has no nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain, and his vital signs are stable.$rw4$::text, $rw4$Which action should the nurse take now?$rw4$::text, $rw4${"kind": "mcq", "options": ["Give 6 units of insulin aspart subcutaneously before the meal", "Give 10 units of insulin aspart subcutaneously before the meal", "Give 8 units of insulin aspart subcutaneously before the meal", "Hold the aspart dose and ask the provider to restart the metformin"], "top500Drugs": ["Metformin (Glucophage)", "Glipizide (Glucotrol)"], "structureIds": ["spleen", "adrenal-glands", "aorta"], "memoryCardIds": ["nclex-pri-abc-first", "nclex-pri-see-first", "nclex-pri-assess-act", "nclex-pri-trap-eventually"], "blueprintTopic": "endocrine-meds", "generationMeta": {"model": "gpt-4o", "batchId": "nclex-gap-2026-07-05-5lymgo", "qcScore": 1, "slotIndex": 14, "examNumber": 9002, "generatedAt": "2026-07-05T07:30:19.289Z", "pipelineVersion": "gpt-4o-nclex-full-exam-v2"}, "blueprintSystem": "nclex-physiological", "reviewModuleSlug": "prioritization", "clinicalReasoning": "Add the correction-scale units for the measured glucose band to the ordered meal dose, verify per high-alert policy, and give it before the meal.", "clientNeedsCategory": "Pharmacological Therapies", "distractorRationale": {"Give 6 units of insulin aspart subcutaneously before the meal": "Gives the fixed meal dose but leaves out the 2-unit correction ordered for a glucose of 201-250 mg/dL, so the dose is too low.", "Give 10 units of insulin aspart subcutaneously before the meal": "Applies the correction for 251-300 mg/dL (4 units) instead of 201-250 mg/dL (2 units), which gives 2 units too many; an overdose of a high-alert drug risks hypoglycemia.", "Hold the aspart dose and ask the provider to restart the metformin": "Metformin is on hold during the admission, and a glucose of 250 mg/dL with no ketosis symptoms calls for the ordered insulin, not withholding it."}, "keyTakeaways": ["Add the correction-scale units for the measured glucose band to the ordered meal dose, verify per high-alert policy, and give it before the meal."]}$rw4$::text, $rw4$Give 8 units of insulin aspart subcutaneously before the meal$rw4$::text, $rw4$## Why this answer is correct
The orders are a basal-prandial-correction plan. At 0730 the glucose is 250 mg/dL, which falls in the 201-250 mg/dL band, so the correction is 2 units. Meal dose 6 units + correction 2 units = **8 units of insulin aspart**, given subcutaneously before the breakfast tray is eaten. The glargine was already given last evening and is not repeated.

**Clinical judgment (NCSBN CJMM):** Recognize cues: glucose 250 mg/dL, thirst, fatigue, no nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain, stable vital signs. Analyze cues: persistent hyperglycemia on held oral agents without signs of ketoacidosis. Prioritize hypotheses: hyperglycemia that the ordered insulin plan is built to treat. Generate solutions: give the ordered insulin. Take action: calculate the dose from the order and the glucose band, verify per facility high-alert-medication policy, and give it. Evaluate outcomes: recheck glucose before the next meal and watch for hypoglycemia.

## Why the other options are wrong
**6 units:** omits the 2-unit correction.
**10 units:** uses the wrong correction band (4 units, for 251-300 mg/dL).
**Hold aspart and ask to restart metformin:** the oral agents are on hold by order, and withholding the ordered insulin leaves the hyperglycemia untreated.

## Guideline basis
ADA Standards of Care 2026, section 16: insulin (and/or other therapy) is started for persistent hyperglycemia at or above 180 mg/dL (16.4b); a basal, prandial, and correction insulin plan is preferred for most non-critically ill inpatients who are eating (16.9); correction insulin alone without basal insulin is discouraged (16.10). The stem's order set therefore follows the current inpatient standard, and the item tests safe calculation and administration against that order.$rw4$::text, $rw4$[{"label":"ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes-2026, Section 16 Diabetes Care in the Hospital (recs 16.4b, 16.9, 16.10), https://diabetesjournals.org/care/article/49/Supplement_1/S339/163925/16-Diabetes-Care-in-the-Hospital-Standards-of-Care"},{"label":"NCSBN 2026 NCLEX-RN Test Plan (Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies: perform calculations needed for medication administration; handle and administer high-risk medications safely), https://ncsbn.org/public-files/2026_RN_Test-Plan_English-F.pdf"}]$rw4$::jsonb, 2::smallint, $rw4$7116036768193c8d6c7dc3e62d4ece719819f2cf28bfe6b9742714b286622148$rw4$::text, $rw4${"memoryHook":"Meal dose + correction band = total aspart dose.","whyCorrect":{"headline":"Give 8 units of insulin aspart subcutaneously before the meal","clinicalContext":"Add the correction-scale units for the measured glucose band to the ordered meal dose, verify per high-alert policy, and give it before the meal."},"whyIncorrect":[{"option":"Give 6 units of insulin aspart subcutaneously before the meal","correction":"Gives the fixed meal dose but leaves out the 2-unit correction ordered for a glucose of 201-250 mg/dL, so the dose is too low."},{"option":"Give 10 units of insulin aspart subcutaneously before the meal","correction":"Applies the correction for 251-300 mg/dL (4 units) instead of 201-250 mg/dL (2 units), which gives 2 units too many; an overdose of a high-alert drug risks hypoglycemia."},{"option":"Hold the aspart dose and ask the provider to restart the metformin","correction":"Metformin is on hold during the admission, and a glucose of 250 mg/dL with no ketosis symptoms calls for the ordered insulin, not withholding it."}],"keyTakeaway":"Add the correction-scale units for the measured glucose band to the ordered meal dose, verify per high-alert policy, and give it before the meal.","source":"ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes-2026, Section 16 Diabetes Care in the Hospital (recs 16.4b, 16.9, 16.10), https://diabetesjournals.org/care/article/49/Supplement_1/S339/163925/16-Diabetes-Care-in-the-Hospital-Standards-of-Care; NCSBN 2026 NCLEX-RN Test Plan (Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies: perform calculations needed for medication administration; handle and administer high-risk medications safely), https://ncsbn.org/public-files/2026_RN_Test-Plan_English-F.pdf"}$rw4$::jsonb, NULL::jsonb),
  ($rw4$cmr770g4d00ua1ywuerwp5c23$rw4$::text, $rw4$nursing$rw4$::text, $rw4$psychosocial$rw4$::text, $rw4$A 30-year-old woman comes to the emergency department with a fractured arm. She says her partner pushed her down the stairs. She is anxious, avoids eye contact, and has multiple bruises in various stages of healing. She is alert, oriented, and able to make her own decisions. Her partner stays at the bedside and answers questions for her. State law does not require clinicians to report partner violence involving a competent adult.$rw4$::text, $rw4$Which nursing action is most appropriate?$rw4$::text, $rw4${"options": ["Document the injuries objectively, assess her safety in private, and report to adult protective services before she agrees", "Document the injuries objectively, assess her safety in private, and offer a social work consult and community resources", "Document the injuries objectively, assess her safety in private, and direct her to a shelter instead of returning home", "Document the injuries objectively, ask about her safety with her partner present, and offer social work and community resources"], "distractorRationale": {"Document the injuries objectively, assess her safety in private, and report to adult protective services before she agrees": "Wrong for one reason: the report goes to adult protective services before she has agreed. State law does not require one here, and the AMA opposes mandatory identifying reports for competent non-elderly adult survivors; filing one over her decision removes her say in the plan and can raise her risk.", "Document the injuries objectively, assess her safety in private, and direct her to a shelter instead of returning home": "Wrong for one reason: the referral is a directive. The nurse offers shelter and other options and supports her own decision and plan; telling her where she must go overrides her autonomy.", "Document the injuries objectively, ask about her safety with her partner present, and offer social work and community resources": "Wrong for one reason: the safety screening happens with her partner present. Screening has to be in private; asking in front of a partner who answers for her can raise her danger and will not produce an honest answer."}, "clinicalReasoning": "For a competent adult with partner violence: private safety assessment, objective documentation, and resources/social work; reporting follows state law, not a blanket rule.", "keyTakeaways": ["For a competent adult with partner violence: private safety assessment, objective documentation, and resources/social work; reporting follows state law, not a blanket rule."], "kind": "mcq", "blueprintTopic": "abuse-neglect", "blueprintSystem": "nclex-health-promotion", "generationMeta": {"model": "gpt-4o", "batchId": "nclex-gap-2026-07-05-pa44ed", "qcScore": 0.9200000000000002, "slotIndex": 5, "examNumber": 9018, "generatedAt": "2026-07-05T02:45:27.841Z", "rationaleModel": "gpt-4o-mini", "expertRationale": {"memoryHook": "IPV: believe, document, connect to help.", "whyCorrect": {"headline": "Document the injuries and involve social services.", "clinicalContext": "She has a fractured arm, says her partner pushed her down the stairs, appears anxious, avoids eye contact and has multiple bruises in various stages of healing. The nurse documents the injuries accurately and involves social services so she has safety planning and resources. Reporting of adult partner violence follows state law and facility policy; some states require it.", "conceptBreakdown": ["She has a fractured arm, says her partner pushed her down the stairs, appears anxious, avoids eye contact and has multiple bruises in various stages of healing.", "The nurse documents the injuries accurately and involves social services so she has safety planning and resources. Reporting of adult partner violence follows state law and facility policy; some states require it."]}, "whyIncorrect": [{"option": "Ask the client why she did not leave the abusive situation", "correction": "Asking why she did not leave is judgmental and blames the victim; it damages trust."}, {"option": "Encourage the client to confront her partner about the abuse", "correction": "Confronting the partner can increase her danger; safety planning is done with support."}, {"option": "Advise the client to return home and avoid provoking her partner", "correction": "Sending her home and telling her to avoid provoking her partner blames her and leaves her in danger."}], "keyTakeaway": "Document IPV injuries objectively and connect the client with resources; adult IPV reporting depends on state law, while child and elder abuse are mandatory reports.", "stepByStepReasoning": ["Recognize cues: fracture, disclosed partner violence, bruises in various stages of healing, anxiety", "Analyze cues: intimate partner violence with ongoing risk", "Prioritize: safety and accurate documentation", "Act: document injuries and involve social services per state law and policy", "Evaluate: safety plan and resources in place."], "source": "OpenStax Maternal-Newborn Nursing 9.2 Domestic and Intimate Partner Violence, https://openstax.org/books/maternal-newborn-nursing/pages/9-2-domestic-and-intimate-partner-violence; NCSBN 2026 NCLEX-RN Test Plan, https://ncsbn.org/public-files/2026_RN_Test-Plan_English-F.pdf"}, "pipelineVersion": "gpt-4o-nclex-full-exam-v2", "manualCorrection": true, "rationaleEnrichedAt": "2026-07-17T06:35:33.900Z", "rationaleQualityScore": 100, "expertRationaleVersion": "nclex-expert-v1", "rationaleEnrichFailCount": 0, "rationaleEnrichFailReason": null}, "clientNeedsCategory": "Psychosocial Integrity", "reviewModuleSlug": "prioritization", "memoryCardIds": ["nclex-precaution-types", "nclex-cdiff-soap", "nclex-mrsa-contact", "nclex-vanc-red-man"], "structureIds": ["lungs", "trachea", "adrenal-glands"]}$rw4$::text, $rw4$Document the injuries objectively, assess her safety in private, and offer a social work consult and community resources$rw4$::text, $rw4$## Why this answer is correct
She is a competent adult with injuries consistent with partner violence, and her partner is controlling the conversation. The nurse documents the injuries objectively, speaks with her **in private** about safety and risk, and **offers social work and community resources** (including a safety plan and shelter or hotline information) so that she can decide what to do. In this scenario state law does not mandate a report, so the nurse does not file one over her decision.

**Clinical judgment (NCSBN CJMM):** Recognize cues: fractured arm, bruises in various stages of healing, disclosure of being pushed, anxiety, partner answering for her. Analyze cues: injuries and behavior consistent with intimate partner violence and ongoing risk. Prioritize hypotheses: her immediate safety and ability to speak freely. Generate solutions: separate her from her partner, document, offer resources and a social work consult. Take action: private safety assessment and referral. Evaluate outcomes: she has a safety plan and resources she can use.

## Why the other options are wrong
**Report to adult protective services before she agrees:** not required in this scenario, and it removes the client's input; reporting duties for adult partner violence vary by state, while child and elder abuse reporting is mandatory.
**Direct her to a shelter instead of returning home:** directive; the nurse offers options and supports her own decision.
**Ask about safety with her partner present:** screening must be private; the partner is answering for her.

## Source basis
OpenStax Maternal-Newborn Nursing 9.2 (the nurse listens and supports, supplies resources, helps create a safety plan, offers a social work consult, and follows state reporting rules). AMA Policy H-515.965 (physicians should assess safety with the patient, make referrals, give resource lists, and record IPV findings; the AMA opposes mandatory reporting laws for competent, non-elderly adult survivors if reports identify them; child maltreatment reporting is supported, and state elder-abuse reporting laws are supported).$rw4$::text, $rw4$[{"label":"OpenStax Maternal-Newborn Nursing 9.2 Domestic and Intimate Partner Violence, https://openstax.org/books/maternal-newborn-nursing/pages/9-2-domestic-and-intimate-partner-violence"},{"label":"AMA Policy H-515.965 Family and Intimate Partner Violence (last modified 2024), https://policysearch.ama-assn.org/policyfinder/detail/family%20and%20intimate?uri=%2FAMADoc%2FHOD.xml-0-4664.xml"},{"label":"NCSBN 2026 NCLEX-RN Test Plan (Psychosocial Integrity: abuse or neglect), https://ncsbn.org/public-files/2026_RN_Test-Plan_English-F.pdf"}]$rw4$::jsonb, 2::smallint, $rw4$0fa0e19247df8b90488e75575acb1cafa01b4cf21ad81c9d40029eb18b886535$rw4$::text, $rw4${"memoryHook":"IPV: separate, document, connect (no blanket adult report).","whyCorrect":{"headline":"Document the injuries objectively, assess her safety in private, and offer a social work consult and community resources","clinicalContext":"For a competent adult with partner violence: private safety assessment, objective documentation, and resources/social work; reporting follows state law, not a blanket rule."},"whyIncorrect":[{"option":"Document the injuries objectively, assess her safety in private, and report to adult protective services before she agrees","correction":"Wrong for one reason: the report goes to adult protective services before she has agreed. State law does not require one here, and the AMA opposes mandatory identifying reports for competent non-elderly adult survivors; filing one over her decision removes her say in the plan and can raise her risk."},{"option":"Document the injuries objectively, assess her safety in private, and direct her to a shelter instead of returning home","correction":"Wrong for one reason: the referral is a directive. The nurse offers shelter and other options and supports her own decision and plan; telling her where she must go overrides her autonomy."},{"option":"Document the injuries objectively, ask about her safety with her partner present, and offer social work and community resources","correction":"Wrong for one reason: the safety screening happens with her partner present. Screening has to be in private; asking in front of a partner who answers for her can raise her danger and will not produce an honest answer."}],"keyTakeaway":"For a competent adult with partner violence: private safety assessment, objective documentation, and resources/social work; reporting follows state law, not a blanket rule.","source":"OpenStax Maternal-Newborn Nursing 9.2 Domestic and Intimate Partner Violence, https://openstax.org/books/maternal-newborn-nursing/pages/9-2-domestic-and-intimate-partner-violence; AMA Policy H-515.965 Family and Intimate Partner Violence (last modified 2024), https://policysearch.ama-assn.org/policyfinder/detail/family%20and%20intimate?uri=%2FAMADoc%2FHOD.xml-0-4664.xml; NCSBN 2026 NCLEX-RN Test Plan (Psychosocial Integrity: abuse or neglect), https://ncsbn.org/public-files/2026_RN_Test-Plan_English-F.pdf"}$rw4$::jsonb, NULL::jsonb),
  ($rw4$cmshj9yog002g1yorbkxdjnpd$rw4$::text, $rw4$pharmacy$rw4$::text, $rw4$compounding-calculations$rw4$::text, $rw4$A pharmacist needs to prepare 500 mL of a 10% w/v dextrose solution using a 5% w/v dextrose solution and a 50% w/v dextrose solution. Assume the volumes are additive.$rw4$::text, $rw4$Rounded to the nearest mL, which combination of volumes will give a 10% w/v solution?$rw4$::text, $rw4${"options": ["400 mL of 5% and 100 mL of 50%", "56 mL of 5% and 444 mL of 50%", "438 mL of 5% and 62 mL of 50%", "444 mL of 5% and 56 mL of 50%"], "distractorRationale": {"400 mL of 5% and 100 mL of 50%": "Gives 14.0% (400 x 0.05 + 100 x 0.50 = 70 g per 500 mL). Common errors: reading the 8:1 ratio as 8 parts out of 10 (80%/20%), or using C1V1 for the 50% (100 mL) and filling the rest with 5% as if it were water.", "56 mL of 5% and 444 mL of 50%": "Gives 45.0%. The diagonal differences are swapped, so the 50% stock gets the 40 parts and the 5% stock gets the 5 parts.", "438 mL of 5% and 62 mL of 50%": "Gives 10.6%. Treats the 8:1 ratio as 1/8 of the total (500/8 = 62.5 mL of 50%) instead of 1/9 (500/9 = 55.6 mL)."}, "clinicalReasoning": "Alligation: each stock's volume is the OPPOSITE diagonal difference over total parts; verify with a mass-balance check.", "keyTakeaways": ["Alligation: each stock's volume is the OPPOSITE diagonal difference over total parts; verify with a mass-balance check."], "blueprintTopic": "alligation", "blueprintSystem": "naplex-area1-foundations"}$rw4$::text, $rw4$444 mL of 5% and 56 mL of 50%$rw4$::text, $rw4$## Why this answer is correct
Alligation (alternate method): put the desired strength (10%) between the two stocks and subtract diagonally.

- Parts of 5% = 50 - 10 = **40**
- Parts of 50% = 10 - 5 = **5**
- Total parts = 45 (also 50 - 5 = 45)

Volume of 5% = 40/45 x 500 mL = 444.4 mL, rounded to **444 mL**. Volume of 50% = 5/45 x 500 mL = 55.6 mL, rounded to **56 mL**.

**Check (alligation medial):** 444.4 mL x 0.05 = 22.2 g and 55.6 mL x 0.50 = 27.8 g; total 50.0 g in 500 mL = 10.0% w/v. With the rounded volumes: 444 x 0.05 + 56 x 0.50 = 22.2 + 28.0 = 50.2 g / 500 mL = 10.04%.

## Why the other options are wrong
**400 mL / 100 mL:** 14.0% (8:1 read as 80%/20%, or C1V1 on the 50% stock with the remainder treated as diluent).
**56 mL / 444 mL:** 45.0% (diagonals swapped).
**438 mL / 62 mL:** 10.6% (8:1 treated as 1/8 of the total instead of 1/9).

## Source basis
Alligation alternate: parts of the stronger stock = desired - weaker strength; parts of the weaker stock = stronger - desired; volume of each = (its parts / total parts) x final volume (RxMath Lab alligation guide and worked examples, which cite Ansel's Pharmaceutical Calculations and USP <1160>; the same method is in the University of Mustansiriyah pharmaceutical calculation lecture notes). The result is independently verifiable by mass balance (sum of strength x volume = 10% x 500 mL).$rw4$::text, $rw4$[{"label":"RxMath Lab, Alligation Practice Problems and method (cites Ansel's Pharmaceutical Calculations; USP <1160>), https://rxmathlab.com/alligation-practice"},{"label":"Pharmaceutical calculation lecture notes (alligation alternate), University of Mustansiriyah, https://uomustansiriyah.edu.iq/media/lectures/4/4_2021_07_16!07_37_45_PM.pdf"},{"label":"NABP NAPLEX Competency Statement 1.C.5 (calculations), as tagged on the original item"}]$rw4$::jsonb, 2::smallint, $rw4$af8a23f697f2be6dcf213d4ad8f44abfec7915e2eb5b64fb83a63ab637964414$rw4$::text, NULL::jsonb, $rw4${"mnemonic":"Opposite diagonal differences give the parts: 40 parts of 5%, 5 parts of 50%.","whyCorrect":"Alligation: each stock's volume is the OPPOSITE diagonal difference over total parts; verify with a mass-balance check.","keyTakeaway":"Alligation: each stock's volume is the OPPOSITE diagonal difference over total parts; verify with a mass-balance check.","distractorRationales":{"400 mL of 5% and 100 mL of 50%":"Gives 14.0% (400 x 0.05 + 100 x 0.50 = 70 g per 500 mL). Common errors: reading the 8:1 ratio as 8 parts out of 10 (80%/20%), or using C1V1 for the 50% (100 mL) and filling the rest with 5% as if it were water.","56 mL of 5% and 444 mL of 50%":"Gives 45.0%. The diagonal differences are swapped, so the 50% stock gets the 40 parts and the 5% stock gets the 5 parts.","438 mL of 5% and 62 mL of 50%":"Gives 10.6%. Treats the 8:1 ratio as 1/8 of the total (500/8 = 62.5 mL of 50%) instead of 1/9 (500/9 = 55.6 mL)."}}$rw4$::jsonb),
  ($rw4$cmr4bs38g00151ycjtidtao40$rw4$::text, $rw4$pharmacy$rw4$::text, $rw4$infectious-disease-rx$rw4$::text, $rw4$A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and hypertension takes metformin 1000 mg twice daily and lisinopril 20 mg daily and reports taking both every day. His blood pressure averaged 146/92 mm Hg over two clinic visits and matching home readings. A1C is 7.8%, eGFR is 88 mL/min/1.73 m2, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 12 mg/g, and potassium is 4.4 mEq/L. He has no history of coronary artery disease, myocardial infarction, angina, or heart failure.$rw4$::text, $rw4$Which medication should the pharmacist recommend adding to his regimen?$rw4$::text, $rw4${"options": ["Amlodipine 5 mg by mouth once daily", "Losartan 50 mg by mouth once daily", "Atenolol 50 mg by mouth once daily", "Spironolactone 25 mg by mouth once daily"], "distractorRationale": {"Losartan 50 mg by mouth once daily": "ACE inhibitor + ARB is dual RAS blockade: the losartan label (section 7.4) warns of hypotension, syncope, hyperkalemia, and acute renal failure with no added benefit (VA NEPHRON-D), and ADA 2026 rec 10.9 says to avoid any ACEi/ARB combination.", "Atenolol 50 mg by mouth once daily": "Beta-blockers are indicated after MI, for active angina, or HFrEF; without those they are not first-line for BP (ADA 2026 sec. 10; 2025 AHA/ACC) and he has none of them.", "Spironolactone 25 mg by mouth once daily": "An MRA is reserved for resistant hypertension on three classes including a diuretic (ADA 2026 rec 10.13) and adds hyperkalemia risk on top of an ACE inhibitor; he is on only one antihypertensive."}, "clinicalReasoning": "Add a DHP calcium channel blocker or thiazide-like diuretic to an ACE inhibitor; never add an ARB to it (dual RAS blockade).", "keyTakeaways": ["Add a DHP calcium channel blocker or thiazide-like diuretic to an ACE inhibitor; never add an ARB to it (dual RAS blockade)."], "kind": "mcq", "blueprintTopic": "hypertension", "blueprintSystem": "naplex-2026-pharmacotherapy", "generationMeta": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "batchId": "naplex-full-2026-07-03-ttz1s1", "qcScore": 0.8999999999999999, "slotIndex": 61, "examNumber": 1, "generatedAt": "2026-07-03T02:31:53.802Z", "rationaleModel": "gpt-4o-mini", "pipelineVersion": "gpt-4o-mini-naplex-full-exam-v1", "rationaleEnrichedAt": "2026-07-17T11:25:11.129Z", "rationaleQualityScore": 100}, "blueprintArea": "naplex-2026-pharmacotherapy"}$rw4$::text, $rw4$Amlodipine 5 mg by mouth once daily$rw4$::text, $rw4$## Why this answer is correct
His blood pressure is above the <130/80 mm Hg goal on a single agent (lisinopril 20 mg) with reliable adherence, so a second drug from a class proven to reduce cardiovascular events in diabetes should be added. He has no albuminuria (UACR 12 mg/g) and no coronary disease, so there is no reason to prefer further RAS blockade. A **dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker** such as amlodipine is a guideline-supported first-line class with a complementary mechanism to an ACE inhibitor, and amlodipine 5 mg daily is a standard starting dose.

**Note for reviewers:** a long-acting thiazide-like diuretic (chlorthalidone, indapamide) is an equally guideline-supported add-on and is intentionally not offered, so that exactly one option is defensible.

## Why the other options are wrong
**Losartan 50 mg:** he is already on an ACE inhibitor; adding an ARB is dual RAS blockade. The losartan label (section 7.4) and ADA 2026 recommendation 10.9 advise against it because of hypotension, syncope, hyperkalemia, and acute kidney injury without added benefit (VA NEPHRON-D).
**Atenolol 50 mg:** no compelling indication (prior MI, angina, HFrEF); beta-blockers have not been shown to reduce mortality as BP-lowering agents without these conditions.
**Spironolactone 25 mg:** fourth-line in practice, for resistant hypertension; it raises hyperkalemia risk with an ACE inhibitor.

## Guideline basis
ADA Standards of Care 2026, section 10: goal <130/80; initial classes are ACE inhibitors, ARBs, thiazide-like diuretics, and dihydropyridine CCBs; without albuminuria ACEi/ARB are not superior to thiazide-like diuretics or DHP-CCBs; 'avoid any combination of ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and direct renin inhibitors' (10.9); MRA after three classes (10.13). 2025 AHA/ACC high blood pressure guideline: ACEi, ARB, DHP-CCB, and thiazide-type diuretics are first-line, and beta-blockers need a compelling indication. Losartan DailyMed label, section 7.4 (dual blockade of the RAS).$rw4$::text, $rw4$[{"label":"ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes-2026, Section 10 Cardiovascular Disease and Risk Management (recs 10.4, 10.6-10.9, 10.13), https://diabetesjournals.org/care/article/49/Supplement_1/S216/163933/10-Cardiovascular-Disease-and-Risk-Management"},{"label":"2025 AHA/ACC Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults, https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacc.2025.05.007"},{"label":"Losartan potassium tablets prescribing information (DailyMed), section 7.4 Dual Blockade of the RAS, https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=5edb843a-66ed-4950-9ed0-393e6a5d7c99"},{"label":"NABP NAPLEX Competency Statement 3.C.2, as tagged on the original item"}]$rw4$::jsonb, 3::smallint, $rw4$f7871878368821cf0c4aa2d94e10b6aff7d4c1bee100fd4f3136aad07814ecda$rw4$::text, $rw4${"memoryHook":"ACEi + ARB = no (hyperkalemia, AKI, syncope); ACEi + CCB or thiazide = yes.","whyCorrect":{"headline":"Amlodipine 5 mg by mouth once daily","clinicalContext":"Add a DHP calcium channel blocker or thiazide-like diuretic to an ACE inhibitor; never add an ARB to it (dual RAS blockade)."},"whyIncorrect":[{"option":"Losartan 50 mg by mouth once daily","correction":"ACE inhibitor + ARB is dual RAS blockade: the losartan label (section 7.4) warns of hypotension, syncope, hyperkalemia, and acute renal failure with no added benefit (VA NEPHRON-D), and ADA 2026 rec 10.9 says to avoid any ACEi/ARB combination."},{"option":"Atenolol 50 mg by mouth once daily","correction":"Beta-blockers are indicated after MI, for active angina, or HFrEF; without those they are not first-line for BP (ADA 2026 sec. 10; 2025 AHA/ACC) and he has none of them."},{"option":"Spironolactone 25 mg by mouth once daily","correction":"An MRA is reserved for resistant hypertension on three classes including a diuretic (ADA 2026 rec 10.13) and adds hyperkalemia risk on top of an ACE inhibitor; he is on only one antihypertensive."}],"keyTakeaway":"Add a DHP calcium channel blocker or thiazide-like diuretic to an ACE inhibitor; never add an ARB to it (dual RAS blockade).","source":"ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes-2026, Section 10 Cardiovascular Disease and Risk Management (recs 10.4, 10.6-10.9, 10.13), https://diabetesjournals.org/care/article/49/Supplement_1/S216/163933/10-Cardiovascular-Disease-and-Risk-Management; 2025 AHA/ACC Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults, https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacc.2025.05.007; Losartan potassium tablets prescribing information (DailyMed), section 7.4 Dual Blockade of the RAS, https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=5edb843a-66ed-4950-9ed0-393e6a5d7c99; NABP NAPLEX Competency Statement 3.C.2, as tagged on the original item"}$rw4$::jsonb, NULL::jsonb)
